TY  - JOUR
TI  - Femtosecond Laser Direct Writing Enabling Optical Fiber Micro/Nano Devices for Biomedical Applications (Invited)
AU  - Xie, F.
AU  - Liang, L.
AU  - Cao, Y.
T2  - Laser and Optoelectronics Progress
AB  - Significance The relentless pursuit of precision medicine and minimally invasive diagnostics has created unprecedented demand for biomedical tools that are miniaturized, highly functional, and capable of operating within the complex environment of the human body. Optical fibers, with their inherent advantages of small cross-sections, flexibility, electromagnetic immunity, and excellent biocompatibility, provide an ideal platform for next-generation medical devices. They serve as efficient conduits for delivering light and collecting signals deep within tissues. However, the functionality of conventional optical fibers is inherently limited by their role as passive transmission channels. Traditional fabrication techniques for fiber-optic devices offer limited design freedom, restricted primarily to planar or simple axial modifications, which hinders the integration of complex functionalities. The emergence of femtosecond laser direct writing (FsLDW) technology has initiated a paradigm shift in micro- and nanofabrication, effectively overcoming these limitations. This powerful technique exploits extreme nonlinear optical effects-such as multiphoton absorption and avalanche ionization-induced by ultrafast laser pulses (typically ~10-15 s) with exceptionally high peak power. A defining feature of FsLDW is its threshold effect, which confines laser-matter interactions to a volume significantly smaller than the diffraction limit at the focal point. This enables true three-dimensional processing with nanoscale precision within transparent materials or on their surfaces. Consequently, FsLDW unlocks the potential to transform a standard optical fiber from a passive light conduit into a sophisticated, multifunctional “lab-on-fiber” platform. By directly fabricating intricate micro-optical elements, sensors, and actuators within the fiber core or cladding, or on its end face or sidewall, FsLDW endows the fiber with new capabilities in sensing, imaging, and manipulation while maintaining its slender and flexible form. This technological advancement is enabling powerful new tools for biosensing, ultra-thin endoscopy, and single-cell analysis, paving the way for transformative progress in personalized diagnostics and targeted therapies. Progress The remarkable progress in FsLDW of fiber micro/nano devices can be systematically categorized into two primary domains based on the fabrication mechanism and the spatial location of the functional structures: embedded devices and surfaceappended devices. A comparative overview of their characteristics is summarized in Table 1.1) Embedded fiber devices. These devices are fabricated by inducing localized and permanent refractive index modifications within the fiber's bulk material (fused silica). The underlying principle involves focusing the femtosecond laser beam inside the fiber core or cladding with pulse energy carefully controlled to exceed the material's modification threshold while remaining below the ablation threshold. This induces a highly nonlinear absorption process, leading to microstructural changes such as color center formation, densification, or nanograting formation, which result in a localized increase in refractive index (change in refractive index typically ranging from 10-4 to 10-2). This process enables the direct“writing”of various embedded optical structures. A seminal application is the fabrication of embedded fiber Bragg gratings (FBGs). Unlike conventional ultraviolet (UV)-laser-based methods, FsLDW enables highly flexible point-by-point or line-by-line inscription of FBGs, allowing the creation of complex apodization profiles, such as Gaussian or sinusoidal apodized nanohole gratings [Fig. 2(a)], and facilitating inscription in specialized fibers such as multi-core or large-mode-area fibers. Recent advancements employing high-speed scanning techniques have further enhanced the writing efficiency and material adaptability of FBGs [Fig. 2(b)]. Beyond gratings, FsLDW is extensively used to create embedded interferometers. Mach-Zehnder interferometers (MZIs) can be fabricated by writing two parallel waveguides or by creating a positive refractive index modified zone (PRIMZ) that couples core and cladding modes. Examples include MZIs in coreless fiber for temperature-insensitive refractive index measurement [Fig. 3(a)] and ultra-compact MZIs written along the core-cladding boundary for highly sensitive torsion measurement [Fig. 3(c)]. Another significant development is the integration of FBGs and coupling waveguides within a multi-core fiber, enabling sophisticated applications such as 2D vector displacement sensing [Fig. 3(d)], demonstrating the high level of functional integration achievable with this technology. 2) Surface-appended fiber devices. This category encompasses devices fabricated on the external surfaces of the fiber, primarily using two distinct FsLDW techniques. The first is femtosecond laser ablation, a subtractive process in which laser pulse energy exceeds the material's ablation threshold, leading to precise material removal via processes such as Coulomb explosion. This technique is ideal for creating micro-cavities, grooves, and D-shaped structures on the fiber sidewall for sensing applications. It has been successfully used to fabricate Fabry-Perot interferometers (FPI) and MZIs for detecting various parameters such as refractive index and vibration [Fig. 6(b)], and, when functionalized, for simultaneous temperature and salinity sensing in seawater [Figs. 6(c) and (d)]. The second, and perhaps more versatile, technique is two-photon polymerization (TPP), an additive manufacturing process. TPP relies on the simultaneous absorption of two photons within a photoresist to initiate polymerization exclusively at the laser focus, enabling the three-dimensional printing of complex polymer microstructures with features below 200 nm. On the fiber end-face, TPP has been revolutionary, allowing for the monolithic integration of micro-optical systems such as ring resonators for highly sensitive vapor detection [Fig. 4(a)], ultra-compliant micro-cantilevers that function as nano-force sensors capable of measuring forces on delicate biological structures like butterfly antennae [Fig. 4(b)], and sophisticated FP cavities with integrated components such as rotatable mirrors for enhanced performance [Fig. 4(d)]. Functional materials can be incorporated, for instance, by coating a TPP-printed hollow cantilever with a Pd film for hydrogen sensing [Fig. 4(c)]. On the fiber sidewall, TPP is often applied to tapered microfibers to exploit their strong evanescent field. This enables the printing of various polymer grating structures, including microfiber Bragg gratings (μ -FBG) and fully encapsulated polymer gratings for enhanced refractive index sensitivity [Figs. 5(a) and (d)], as well as helical gratings for polarization-sensitive sensing [Fig. 5(c)]. For improved robustness, polymer gratings can be encapsulated within quartz tubes [Fig. 5(b)]. 3) Biomedical applications. The unique attributes of FsLDW-fabricated devices have catalyzed significant innovations across multiple biomedical fields. 4) Biosensing. The miniaturization and high sensitivity of these devices enable new paradigms in physiological monitoring and diagnostic testing. Examples include a wearable respiratory sensor based on a fiber-tip micro-cantilever, which can be integrated into a face mask for real-time monitoring of breathing patterns, even within magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) environments [Fig. 7(a)]. A fiber-optic nanomotion sensor that detects the metabolic vibrations of microorganisms, enabling rapid antibiotic susceptibility testing within minutes [Fig. 7(b)]. A triaxial force sensor printed on a guidewire tip, providing real-time tactile feedback to physicians during minimally invasive vascular surgeries [Fig. 7(c)]. And a fiber-tip surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) probe fabricated via TPP, enabling label-free, rapid identification of live bacteria [Fig. 7(d)]. 5) Invasive imaging. FsLDW is pivotal for developing the next generation of ultra-thin and high-performance endoscopic probes. Using TPP, complex micro-optical elements such as multi-lens objectives can be directly printed onto a single optical fiber or imaging fiber bundle, significantly improving resolution and field of view [Figs. 8(a) and (b)]. This capability has been directly applied to optical coherence tomography (OCT), leading to the development of miniaturized probes, including common-path designs with off-axis parabolic reflectors [Fig. 8(c)] and ultra-thin side-viewing probes with free-form micro-optics that enable high-resolution cross-sectional imaging of arterial walls in vivo [Fig. 8(d)]. 6) Micro-probe manipulation. FsLDW enables the creation of active and passive micro-manipulators directly on fiber tips. A prominent example is a monolithic micro-gripper that can mechanically grasp individual cells while simultaneously monitoring the applied grip force via an integrated FPI sensor, ensuring safe handling of delicate biological samples [Fig. 9(a)]. Furthermore, optical manipulation is achieved by printing micro-optical elements such as spiral zone plates (SZP) that generate optical vortices (twisted light), enabling non-contact trapping and controlled rotation of individual cells, such as red blood cells, for biomechanical studies [Fig. 9(b)]. Conclusions and Prospects In summary, FsLDW has firmly established itself as a transformative and versatile manufacturing technology for creating advanced optical fiber micro- and nano-devices. Its unparalleled capability for three-dimensional processing with nanoscale precision, both within and on the surface of optical fibers, has enabled the development of a new class of highly sensitive biosensors, ultra-compact imaging probes, and sophisticated micro-manipulators. These devices are already making substantial contributions to biomedical research and hold significant promise for future clinical translation by facilitating minimally invasive and highly precise diagnostic and therapeutic procedures. Looking ahead, the field will evolve along several promising and interdisciplinary trajectories. First, material innovation will be essential. Extending the TPP process beyond conventional polymers to include glass-ceramics, hydrogels, and biocompatible hybrid materials will improve the thermal stability, chemical robustness, and long-term biocompatibility of the devices-critical attributes for implants and chronic indwelling sensors. The recent demonstration of direct 3D nanolithography of silica glass on fiber tips represents a significant advancement in this direction. Second, deeper integration with other technological platforms, such as microfluidics, will enable the development of sophisticated lab-on-fiber systems for multi-parameter, on-chip biochemical analysis of minute fluid samples. Third, the incorporation of artificial intelligence and machine learning will transform the entire workflow-from inverse design of complex micro-optics tailored to specific functions, to optimization of laser writing parameters for consistent, high-quality fabrication, and to intelligent analysis of the complex datasets generated by these sensors. The ultimate goal is the realization of an Allin-One fiber-based theranostic system, in which a single, minimally invasive fiber probe seamlessly integrates real-time physiological sensing (e.g., biomarkers, temperature, pH), high-resolution imaging (e.g., OCT), optical manipulation of cells or drug carriers, and localized light-activated therapy. Overcoming current challenges related to fabrication speed for mass production, the long-term stability of polymer components in vivo, and process standardization will be crucial to transitioning these advanced laboratory prototypes into robust, commercially viable, and clinically approved tools that will fundamentally shape the future of precision medicine. © 2026 Chinese Laser Press. All rights reserved.
