TY  - JOUR
TI  - Femtosecond-Laser-Induced Anisotropy in Glass: From Micro-Nano Structure to Functional Devices (Invited)
AU  - Liu, Y.
AU  - Xiao, Z.
AU  - Fu, Y.
AU  - Yan, Y.
AU  - Hu, J.
AU  - Zhang, J.
T2  - Laser and Optoelectronics Progress
AB  - Significance Femtosecond laser direct writing has emerged as a transformative technique for constructing three-dimensional, highly controllable anisotropic micro-nano structures inside transparent glasses. Owing to its ultrashort pulse duration, extremely high peak intensity, and nonlinear absorption with minimal thermal diffusion, the technique enables localized energy deposition deep within bulk materials, bypassing the intrinsic transparency of glass to linear light. These advantages make femtosecond lasers uniquely powerful for inducing direction-sensitive modifications-such as birefringence, stress anisotropy, and refractive-index asymmetry-that serve as critical building blocks for integrated photonics, multidimensional optical data storage, polarization manipulation, and next-generation optical information processing. As modern photonics rapidly evolves toward miniaturization, high density, and multifunctional integration, the ability to precisely tailor anisotropic structures within dielectric substrates provides both fundamental insights into light-matter interaction and practical pathways for fabricating robust, long-lived all-glass optical devices. Progress Research on femtosecond-laser-induced anisotropy in glass has advanced significantly over the past two decades. The induced structures can be broadly categorized into periodic and aperiodic anisotropic nanostructures. Periodic nanogratings-first reported as self-organized subwavelength lamellae oriented perpendicular to laser polarization-remain the most widely investigated structures (Fig. 1). Their geometric characteristics, including period, duty cycle, and orientation, can be tuned across various glass compositions, laser wavelengths, and pulse durations. Further studies reveal more complex crystalline-amorphous hybrid periodic structures formed in multicomponent glasses, where periodic crystalline lamellae emerge via polarization-dependent or scan-directiondependent mechanisms (Fig. 2). These discoveries expand the classical interference-based model and motivated the development of advanced mechanisms such as nanopore-mediated interference and self-organized phase-transition lithography. Aperiodic anisotropic structures have attracted increasing interest due to their superior optical performance and ultralow scattering loss. Representative examples include Type X nanoporous structures in fused silica, which maintain more than 99% transmittance across the visible and near-infrared range (Fig. 3), and single-nanopore slits with widths down to 20-40 nm (Fig. 4). These structures exhibit polarization-dependent or spatiotemporally modulated growth dynamics, enabling new functionalities in high-speed and high-density data storage. Recent innovations-such as near-field-enhanced nanolamella formation, temporally separated pulse-pair writing, and three-pulse ultrafast structuring-have substantially reduced the number of pulses required to create anisotropic features, raising the writing speed to MB/s level. Beyond fused silica, anisotropic nanostructures have been reported in lithium-aluminosilicate and alkaline-earth boroaluminosilicate glasses (Fig. 5). These materials support low-loss nanocrystal-based birefringent structures with high transparency (up to 99.7%), offering promising pathways toward large-scale, high-durability polarization-sensitive optical components. In parallel, theoretical understanding has progressed from classical interference models to more comprehensive frameworks that integrate plasma dynamics, surface plasmon resonance, and near-field enhancement. Interference-based models successfully explain the orientation and periodicity of nanogratings, whereas near-field enhancement models clarify anisotropic growth within nanopore precursors and the evolution from nanoplasmas to nanolamellae. Meanwhile, spatiotemporal pulse-shaping experiments have introduced new mechanisms not fully captured by classical theories, suggesting the need for unified models encompassing multipulse accumulation, defect dynamics, and ultrafast phase transitions (Fig. 6 and Fig. 8). Applications of femtosecond-laser-induced anisotropic structures have expanded rapidly. In micro-nano optical elements, periodic nanogratings have enabled on-chip waveplates, polarization gratings, geometric-phase holograms, near-infrared attenuators, and fiber-integrated photonic components (Fig. 9). Aperiodic structures such as Type X nanopores and low-loss nanocrystals have further enhanced device transparency and performance. In data storage, 5D optical memory systems exploiting spatial coordinates (x, y, z) and birefringent parameters (retardance and slow-axis orientation) have demonstrated ultrahigh capacities exceeding hundreds of TB per disk (Figs. 10-11). Recent breakthroughs, including watt-type voxel stacking, parallel writing, and multipulse-modulated recording, have pushed storage accuracy, density, and speed toward practical commercialization. Conclusions and Prospects Femtosecond-laser-induced anisotropy in glass constitutes a rapidly developing frontier that integrates ultrafast photonics, materials science, and nanofabrication. Periodic and aperiodic anisotropic structures provide complementary advantages-structural regularity and stability for photonic components, and nanoscale dimensions with minimal optical loss for data storage and advanced polarization manipulation. Despite substantial progress, several challenges remain: the formation mechanisms of different anisotropic structures lack a unified theoretical framework; multiphysics coupling processes such as plasma evolution, defect generation, and stress dynamics require deeper elucidation; and stable, predictable control over structure morphology across material systems remains difficult. Looking ahead, progress is expected along three major directions. First, mechanistic unification through combined ultrafast spectroscopy, multiphysics modeling, and machine-learning-assisted analysis will clarify the interplay among interference, plasma dynamics, near-field effects, and phase transitions. Second, precision control via adaptive pulse shaping, spatiotemporal beam engineering, and feedback-controlled writing will enhance reproducibility and enable deterministic tailoring of anisotropy. Third, application expansion will integrate anisotropic structures into multifunctional photonics, ultralong-term data storage, extreme-environment sensing, and quantum information platforms. The synergy of these advances will push femtosecond laser nanofabrication toward higher integration density, greater functionality, and broader engineering applicability. © 2026 Chinese Laser Press. All rights reserved.
