AU  - Ferraresi, C.
AU  - Cappuzzello, F.
AU  - Cavallaro, M.
T2  - Nuclear Instruments and Methods in Physics Research, Section A: Accelerators, Spectrometers, Detectors and Associated Equipment
AB  - The NUMEN experiment will use the upgraded version of the MAGNEX spectrometer designed to withstand higher intensity ion beams coming from the refurbished superconducting cyclotron of the Laboratori Nazionali del Sud of INFN in Catania. This large acceptance magnetic spectrometer accepts ejectiles from the reactions between heavy ions beams and targets which occur in a custom scattering chamber fulfilling the requirements of the experiment. The NUMEN goal is to study heavy ion induced double charge exchange reactions on isotopes of interest for the neutrino-less double beta decays studies and bring information of the Nuclear Matrix Elements being the two processes similar in some aspects. The scattering chamber hosts a target, cooled down with a system based on a material featuring high thermal conductivity and a cryo-cooler, and a Faraday cup to measure the beam charge. Around the chamber, gamma rays detectors cover about the 70 % of the entire solid angle. Upstream, the connection to the beam line allows for different configurations and downstream, the slits chamber to shape the ejectiles acceptance of the spectrometer. On one side the manipulator enters the chamber to replace an exhausted or broken target. Design, construction and functionality of the custom scattering chamber with its equipment will be presented in this paper. © 2025
