TI  - Autonomous Weeding Robots: Coordinating Locomotion and Manipulation via Long Short-Term Memory-Proximal Policy Optimization Reinforcement Learning
AU  - Ma, S.
AU  - Li, R.
AU  - Cong, S.
AU  - Nie, X.
AU  - Khan, R.A.I.
AU  - Yu, C.
AU  - Zhou, W.
AU  - Hu, H.
AU  - Fang, H.
AU  - Shang, H.
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering
AB  - Autonomous weeding robots offer significant potential for reducing labor and environmental costs in precision agriculture. However, most systems decouple chassis locomotion from manipulator control, limiting efficiency in unstructured environments. We present LSTM-PPO-Weeding, an end-to-end framework unifying mobility and manipulation via memory-augmented reinforcement learning (RL). A high-speed region of interest (ROI) filter abstracts camera input into three-dimensional weed/crop poses, enabling the system to focus solely on task-relevant features. To mitigate challenges arising from occlusion and partial observability, we integrate long short-term memory (LSTM) into the proximal policy optimization (PPO) algorithm, establishing the proposed LSTM-PPO framework. The agent learns coordinated actions through a custom reward function balancing speed and precision. Experiments in simulation and real-world greenhouse settings show that LSTM-PPO-Weeding improves throughput while maintaining high accuracy. Compared to baseline methods, our approach reduces average weeding time by up to 74% with minimal loss in success rate, demonstrating its robustness for agricultural mobile manipulation. Note to Practitioners - This work was motivated by the need to enhance the operational speed and adaptability of autonomous weeding robots in real-world agricultural settings. Conventional robotic systems typically decouple locomotion and weeding into distinct steps - either stopping to weed or operating at fixed speeds - which limits efficiency, particularly in greenhouses or row crops with variable weed distributions. This paper introduces a control framework that enables a robot to simultaneously move and weed using a memory-enhanced RL method. The system integrates a camera-based region of interest (ROI) filter to identify task-relevant plant features and a memory-based control strategy to manage occlusions and uncertain field conditions. These components allow the robot to make informed, coordinated decisions in real time. Tested in greenhouse environments, our approach demonstrated a 74% reduction in average weeding time compared to traditional methods, with minimal loss in accuracy. It requires no manually tuned speed profiles and adapts effectively to changing weed layouts. However, the current system assumes relatively smooth terrain and does not yet include recovery mechanisms for scenarios such as wheel entrapment or severe misalignment. This framework can be adapted for broader agricultural tasks, such as spraying, harvesting, or warehouse automation, where coordinated mobility and manipulation under partial visibility are required.  © 2026 IEEE.
