TI  - PPoison: A Pluggable Poisoning attack against distributed training of split learning
AU  - Liu, J.
AU  - Lyu, X.
AU  - Zheng, L.
AU  - Ren, C.
AU  - Cui, Q.
T2  - Future Generation Computer Systems
AB  - Although the private data are maintained locally by the participants, split learning (SL) is also vulnerable to different (e.g., privacy and poisoning) attacks, hindering its practical deployment. Targeted poisoning attack is one of the critical security concerns to SL, where the adversary can intentionally control the output to a specified label after the training. However, existing attacks may be inefficient due to the incomplete model knowledge in the split setting and complicated trigger design. We propose an efficient and versatile Pluggable Poisoning attack, PPoison, against SL. We append an extra trigger layer to the local model to manipulate the embeddings. After being trained via limited label information, the trigger layer can transform benign embeddings to be classified to the target label. PPoison can keep robust poisoning performance under different settings. We evaluate the proposed PPoison on seven different datasets with four distinct data formats. Experimental results validate the superiority of PPoison with close to 100% poisoning success rate and little accuracy degradation. Moreover, we found that multiple malicious parties can cause a significant threat by joint poisoning. We further evaluate four potential defense strategies, which are shown not to provide comprehensive defense performance, necessitating the design of advanced defense techniques. © 2025
