TY  - JOUR
TI  - Feelings over reasons: The impact of exposure to nature on consumer decision making
AU  - Xu, S.
AU  - Ding, Y.
T2  - Journal of Business Research
AB  - Despite the ubiquity of natural elements in consumers’ lives, their influence on decision making has received limited attention. This research examines how and why exposure to nature shapes the way consumers make decisions. Across seven experiments, we find converging evidence that consumers exposed to nature are more likely to rely on feelings rather than reasons, leading to a stronger preference for affectively (vs. cognitively) superior product options and greater scope insensitivity. This shift occurs because exposure to nature decreases consumers’ self-monitoring motivation. We validate these findings through multiple manipulation methods and choice contexts. Furthermore, we identify decision target (i.e., making decisions for oneself or for others) as an important boundary condition that further supports our conceptualization. Theoretically, this research advances understanding of how exposure to nature influences consumer preference. Practically, it provides valuable insights for firms pursuing business model innovation, particularly in retail management. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
