TI  - Engineering of Escherichia coli for high production of poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) from a glycerol-xylose mixture
AU  - Luu, N.L.
AU  - Ta, D.T.
AU  - Chiang, C.-J.
AU  - Chao, Y.-P.
T2  - Journal of the Taiwan Institute of Chemical Engineers
AB  - Background Petroleum-based plastics are not carbon-neutral products and contradict the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) (PHB) is recognized as a promising alternative to conventional plastics. Crude glycerol and xylose are carbon-neutral, renewable, and inexpensive feedstocks. To achieve the SDGs, a sustainable PHB production process was developed using Escherichia coli with crude glycerol and raw xylose. Methods The PHB-producing strain was developed by manipulating the xylose transporter and dissimilation pathway. The pentose phosphate pathway was further rewired in the strain. As a result, the engineered strain enabled efficient co-utilization of glycerol and xylose. The fermentation process was subsequently operated in a fed-batch mode to produce PHB at large scale. Significant findings The microbial fermentation was carried out by using xylose to grow the producer strain, followed by feeding the glycerol-xylose mixture. It resulted in the PHB titer, content, and productivity reaching 60.1 g/L, 89 %, and 2.1 g/L/h, respectively. The PHB yield on glycerol and xylose accounts for 0.39 g/g. The results indicate that the technology platform developed in this study is promising for producing bioplastics. Overall, this study may open a new avenue for the mass production of bio-based products using a glycerol-xylose mixture in biorefineries. © 2026 Taiwan Institute of Chemical Engineers.
