TY  - JOUR
TI  - Technovation and consumer dynamics: bridging digital transformation and sustainable growth through AI anchors on DD Kisan
AU  - Sardar, S.
AU  - Chaudhary, K.
T2  - Organizational Resilience and Leadership issues in the dynamic era
AB  - While many parts of India are still using traditional modes of production, the country is rapidly emerging as a digital superpower and a forerunner of sustainable growth. For consumers that comprise approximately half of the Indian population and rely on one of the oldest professions of the nation—farming, this has been a game changer. Today, approximately 42.3 % of the Indian population depends on agriculture for its livelihood, with 46% of the total Indian workers employed in agriculture (Economic Survey Report of 2023–24). Despite the promising data, the majority of farmers in India are poor. Apart from factors like failure to own large land portions, low budgetary access, lack of resources for the preservation of crops, dependency on the natural rain cycle, and transportation problems (inability to book vehicles for carrying crops or poor road infrastructure), the productivity and crop quality of Indian farmers are often compromised owing to their lack of information regarding modern irrigation techniques, government-aided schemes, and climate-smart agricultural practices. To mitigate this issue, in May 2015, the Indian government began broadcasting agriculture-based information through DD Kisan, a flagship channel of Doordarshan, under the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting. One of the programs broadcasting on the channel is Krishi Darshan, which first premiered on 26th January 1967 and is one of the longest-running programs in the country that has helped innumerable farmers learn about new-age irrigation techniques, water harvesting, and additive-free farming. Keeping pace with the global digital revolution and ensuring the country’s sustainable development while catering to one of the most critical consumer cohorts of India, i.e., farmers, the government deployed two AI anchors, namely AI Krish and AI Bhoomi, on Sunday, the 26th of May 2024, on Krishi Darshan. The anchors are programmed to speak in 50 national and international languages, thereby easing the broadcasting of the program in every Indian state in each of their respective regional languages. The origin of this trend could be traced to China, when the official state news agency Xinhua paired up with a Chinese technological company, Sogou, to develop an array of AI news anchors like Qiu Hao that fashioned human news anchors from the agency. These AI bots, acclaimed to be the world’s first AI news anchors, were presented at the World Internet Conference in 2018. Soon India followed suit, and in March 2023, witnessed the first AI anchor, Sana, launched by the India Today group, followed by AIRA, the AI news anchor of ABP News. Taking the clue, Odisha’s News Channel OTV introduced Lisa, the first regional AI-generated news anchor of the nation, succeeded by Saundarya, the bot anchor of Power TV. When juxtaposed with their human counterparts, these are several captivating attributes of the bot anchors in correlation with the consumer effect that outweighs the importance of human news anchors. From customizable avatars to non-stop delivery of updated news with unbeatable accuracy and a huge reduction in the cost of multiple work shifts, teleprompting, video modifications, and other production-related expenses, AI news anchors can leverage natural language processing (NLP) and deep learning techniques to foster phenomenal results. However, irrespective of efforts to replicate human expressions, the voice, tone, and bearings of the bots clearly depict artificial synthesis. The illusion can be easily detected by human senses and cannot beat field reporting and mediation of televised altercations, traditionally executed by human anchors. It’s no wonder then that for now, AI TV anchors like Sana and Lisa are used to reading headlines, following which the news delivery is passed on to their human peers. Journalistic risk is another unsettling concern that media houses take into consideration while using anchors of generative AI. Devoid of a natural and humane moral compass like conscience, these bots are subject to data security breaches and can be used by criminal groups to spread global anarchy through misinformation. Be that as it may, with five bot news anchors launched in less than a year, India is witnessing rapid digitization in the Technovation of Media space. Thus, the question remains: To what extent will AI news anchors replace humans in the times to come? According to Sevanti Ninan, anchor and founder of ‘The Hoot’, the Indian media watchdog, “AI chatbots will save money and can be used for headlines, but I don’t see how they will be able to replace a reporter out in the field asking questions.” “It’s too early to tell what they’ll do to the concept of star anchors” (Ninan, 2023). John Southurst, the Tokyo-based author of the media group CoinGeek, begs to differ: “We shouldn’t assume that because some technology doesn’t work very well right now, it never will. It seems like common sense, but a lot of people continue to make that mistake. Open AI will iron out any problems with GPT if they decide it’s impeding the project. Maybe they will provide different versions of it for different clients, depending on the type of content they need to produce. In any case, aside from any debate over whether machine learning and LLMs are forms of “intelligence” or not, it’d be unwise to judge their future performance on current examples. Assume that someday, and someday soon, generative AI will be able to produce convincing news content. Journalists working today will just have to deal with it” [1]. This paper dives into the changing dynamics that the two AI-generated news anchors of DD Kisan are bringing to the forefront regarding the 50+ years of parasocial relationships between Indian farmers and human news anchors. The purpose is to highlight concerns like Man vs. Machine in the consumer engagement success ratio, effects on audiences of various levels of social overload, and the extent to which AI anchors can mimic human behaviors. An in-depth analysis of the potential of these bots in establishing long-term emotional connections with their viewers for transformative outputs will also be conducted. Since the primary purpose of employing the bots has been to enhance consumer experience while palliating the surging cost of production budgets, the study will also throw light on the extent of fiscal advantages attained through their application and the change in consumer dynamics after their utilization, along with the benefits and drawbacks associated with the ground-breaking technovation of AI-generated news anchors. The methodology employed would be qualitative in nature with case studies, interviews, recorded videos, and literature reviews used extensively. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Deepti Dabas Hazarika, Arnab Chakraborty, Shiwangi Sharma, Apurva A. Chauhan and Neha Yadav; individual chapters, the contributors
