TI  - Shaping the future: board communication, credit risk and blockchain technology evolution in banking industries – evidence from Bangladesh
AU  - Islam, M.M.
AU  - Islam, M.M.
AU  - Islam, M.A.
AU  - Alharthi, M.
AU  - Hassan, M.S.
T2  - International Journal of Islamic and Middle Eastern Finance and Management
AB  - Purpose – Effective board communication and blockchain technology are essential for managing credit risk in today’s banking landscape. This study aims to investigate the influence of board smart communication on default risk, with blockchain technology examined as a mediating factor in this relationship. Design/methodology/approach – This study uses structural equation modeling (SEM) using STATA version 13 to analyze both primary and secondary data. Primary data were collected through structured interviews, whereas secondary data were gathered from the annual reports of 20 Bangladeshi banks over a six-year period. Findings – The results reveal a significant positive relationship between board smart communication and default risk. In addition, blockchain technology is found to mediate this relationship, suggesting that its implementation can mitigate the negative effects of poor board communication on credit risk. Research limitations/implications – The paper also provides suggestions for disguising the blockchain traffic profile, which will make identifying and communicating with it on the data network much more difficult. Practical implications – Using blockchain technology is very important for controlling credit risk in developing countries like Bangladesh. These countries often face problems such as high inflation, falling currency value and poor management practices, which can make it hard for people to repay loans. Blockchain provides a secure and unchangeable system for recording data, and smart contracts can help carry out tasks automatically and fairly. This makes daily banking activities more transparent and trustworthy, helping to reduce fraud, corruption and false information that can lead to loan defaults. Originality/value – This paper fills a gap in the literature by empirically examining the mediating role of blockchain technology between board communication and credit risk. Few studies have explored this triadic relationship, especially in the context of developing economies. The findings offer novel insights into governance and technological innovation in the banking sector. © 2025 Emerald Publishing Limited
