TI  - Detecting Ship-to-Ship Transfer by MOSA: Multi-Source Observation Framework with SAR and AIS
AU  - Cai, P.
AU  - Liu, B.
AU  - Li, X.
AU  - Li, X.
AU  - Wang, S.
AU  - Liu, P.
AU  - Chen, P.
AU  - Li, Y.
T2  - Remote Sensing
AB  - Highlights: What are the main findings? A Multi-source Observation framework with SAR and AIS (MOSA) for ship-to-ship (STS) transfer monitoring is proposed, compirising an SAR-based fine-grained ship detection network (STS-YOLO) and an SAR-AIS Consistency Analysis Workflow (SACA-Workflow). Experimental results across multiple scenes demonstrate that STS-YOLO achieves higher accuracy in fine-grained ship detection than representative baseline methods, and the SACA-Workflow enables effective identification of suspected abnormal ship-to-ship transfer. What are the implications of the main findings? A new perspective for ship-to-ship transfer identification through the integration of SAR imagery and AIS data. Enhanced support for maritime supervision by enabling the identification of suspected abnormal STS events and potential AIS inconsistency. Ship-to-ship (STS) transfer has become a major concern for maritime security and regulatory authorities, as it is frequently exploited for smuggling and other illicit activities. Accurate and timely identification of STS events is therefore essential for effective maritime supervision. Existing monitoring approaches, however, suffer from two inherent limitations: AIS-based surveillance is vulnerable to intentional signal shutdown or manipulation, and remote-sensing-based ship detection alone lacks digital identity information and cannot assess the legitimacy of transfer activities. To address these challenges, we propose a Multi-source Observation framework with SAR and AIS (MOSA), which integrates SAR imagery with AIS data. The framework consists of two key components: STS-YOLO, a high-precision fine-grained ship detection model, in which a dynamic adaptive feature extraction (DAFE) module and a multi-attention mechanism (MAM) are introduced to enhance feature representation and robustness in complex maritime SAR scenes, and the SAR-AIS Consistency Analysis Workflow (SACA-Workflow), designed to identify suspected abnormal STS behaviors by analyzing inconsistencies between physical and digital ship identities. Experimental results on the SDFSD-v1.5 dataset demonstrate the quantitative performance gains and improved fine-grained detection performance of STS-YOLO in terms of standard detection metrics. In addition, generalization experiments conducted on large-scene SAR imagery from the waters near Panama and Singapore, in addition to multi-satellite SAR data (Capella Space and Umbra) from the Gibraltar region, validate the cross-regional and cross-sensor robustness of the proposed framework. The effectiveness of the SACA-Workflow is evaluated qualitatively through representative case studies. In all evaluated scenarios, the SACA-Workflow effectively assists in identifying suspected abnormal STS events and revealing potential AIS inconsistency indicators. Overall, MOSA provides a robust and practical solution for multi-scenario maritime monitoring and supports reliable detection of suspected abnormal STS activities. © 2026 by the authors.
