TI  - Attack-detector-based control of nonlinear systems against false data injection
AU  - Fu, R.
AU  - Zhang, H.
AU  - Liu, G.
AU  - Ji, H.
AU  - Liang, X.
AU  - Xu, S.
T2  - Applied Mathematics and Computation
AB  - False data injection, a typical form of deception attack, is recognized as more hazardous than denial-of-service attacks. When mounting such an attack, network attackers capture data packets, inject false information, and intentionally disrupt the normal operation of the system — an act that may damage the system's hardware and software or even cause the entire system to collapse. In the case of uncertainty in deception attacks, even if a system observer or filter is introduced, it is impossible to completely eliminate the threat of the attack. In order to eliminate the threat of false data injection, a control strategy based on attack detectors has been proposed between the system output and the feedback controller in this paper. By combining the detector with the system state observer, the reliability of the feedback data can be effectively enhanced, thereby improving the performance of the controller. Considering the uncertainty of the attack, this paper proposes a detection mechanism based on iterative learning. By continuously learning from the residual signals, it further improves the prediction effect on attack signals. Finally, an application for single-link robot arm systems is given to illustrate the validity of the proposed methods. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
