AB  - Purpose: Adapting to talker variability typically incurs a cost to speech pertion. However, it remains unclear whether the underlying mechanism of mtalker processing cost (MTPC) reflects the recomputation of acoustic–phoneinterpretations or the reorientation of auditory attention. This study tests thaccounts in Cantonese lexical tone perception, where lexical tone categorand talker identity are both represented in fundamental frequency (F0). Method: Two tone pairs, one differing primarily in F0 height and one differiF0 contour, were used in a within-subject tone identification task. The numbtalkers was manipulated (one, two, four, and eight), varying the numbers acoustic–phonetic interpretations required. Measures included accuracy, responstime, and an efficiency index. Listeners’ expectations about talker variability walso assessed for the modulation of the cost. Results: As the number of talkers increased from one to two to four, both pairs displayed a gradual increase in MTPC (reduced accuracy, longer respotimes, or lower efficiency). No additional cost was observed from four to talkers. The magnitude of the cost was modulated by expectations. Conclusions: The results suggest that talker adaptation in lexical tone perction is an active process that initially requires recomputation but plateaus increasing talker exposure. Importantly, the underlying mechanisms differ frothat observed in English vowel perception, indicating that talker variability inacts with language-specific phonetic demands. This highlights the need cross-linguistic approaches to models of multitalker processing. © 2026 American Speech-Language-Hearing Association.
