TY  - JOUR
TI  - High-Hydroxyl Nonlinear Optical Fibers and Their Supercontinuum Applications
AU  - Wang, T.
AU  - Yu, S.
AU  - Wang, J.
AU  - Liao, M.
AU  - Xia, W.
AU  - Wang, L.
AU  - He, D.
T2  - Zhongguo Jiguang/Chinese Journal of Lasers
AB  - Objective This research systematically explores hydroxyl impurities in fused silica, with photonic crystal fibers (PCFs) as the core research platform, and aims to manipulate nonlinear optical dynamics through the precise control of hydroxyl groups while focusing on the modulation of supercontinuum (SC) generation performance. This work is motivated by the overlooked potential of high-hydroxyl fibers in the field of nonlinear optics: traditional research prioritizes hydroxyl reduction to minimize optical loss, whereas this study leverages hydroxyl groups as functional elements and investigates the 1380 nm absorption peak for spectral engineering. Its specific objectives include analyzing the enhancement mechanisms of third-order Raman scattering, with targeted applications in biomedicine and optical communications in mind, and the ultraviolet (UV) radiation resistance of high-OH fibers is also a relevant research focus. The ultimate goal is to design novel nonlinear photonic devices, and a critical research task is to validate the hydroxyl-induced “spectral barrier”effect, which addresses the inherent limitations of conventional Raman gain systems. This work thus intentionally builds a bridge between material science and nonlinear photonics. Methods High-OH silica rods are fabricated via the vapor-phase axial deposition (VAD) method, with chlorine-based dehydration employed to control the OH content by adjusting the chlorine gas (Cl2) flow rate and reaction temperature, enabling precise regulation of the hydroxyl concentration. Hydroxyl levels are measured using spectrophotometry on samples with a thickness of 1.34 mm, and the formula Cppm=(K/L) lg(T0/T) is applied with the K value set to 910, achieving a hydroxyl mass fraction of up to 459×10-6. Fibers are produced through stack-and-draw techniques, which yields both high-OH photonic crystal fiber (HH-PCF) and low-OH photonic crystal fiber (LH-PCF) with identical cross-sections, uniform capillary stacking density and consistent drawing parameters under cleanroom conditions exceeding Class 10000. Structural consistency is ensured to minimize experimental variables, with only the hydroxyl content differing between the two fibers. Attenuation is evaluated via the cut-back method with a white light source providing broadband input, where HH-PCF exhibits a loss of 32 dB/m at 1380 nm and LH-PCF shows a loss of 0.32 dB/m at the same wavelength. Pump pulses are generated by a three-stage amplifier system, with the seed laser operating at 1064 nm to produce nanosecond-scale pulses with an average power of 6 W and a peak power of 8 kW. Isolators suppress backward reflections, bandpass filters reduce amplified spontaneous emission, pump strippers remove cladding light, circulators monitor backward light, mode field adapters enable efficient optical coupling, and fiber end-caps prevent terminal damage. The PCF is fixed at a length of 4 m, and the output power and spectra are recorded for characterization. Finite element method is used to model the fiber properties, calculating the effective refractive index and dispersion, and analyzing the four-wave mixing phase matching. Corresponding equations simulate the pump and signal light interactions, while the nonlinear Schrödinger equation, which incorporates dispersion, self-phase modulation (SPM) and Raman effects, describes the light propagation process. Experimental loss data is integrated into the simulations, with the Raman fraction set to 0.18 and the nonlinear coefficient set to 0.036 W-1·m-1. For simulation efficiency, the fiber length is set to 1 m with a peak power of 20 kW, and the spectral evolution of the light field is visualized. Results and Discussions Absorption in HH-PCF at 1380 nm is dominant, with its loss of 32 dB/m far exceeding the 0.32 dB/m of LH-PCF, which confirms the major impact of hydroxyl groups on optical loss. Under the identical average pump power of 6 W, HH-PCF generates 1.348 W SC output while LH-PCF produces a higher 1.992 W, resulting in a total power reduction in HH-PCF. Despite the lower total output power, HH-PCF exhibits a strong Raman response: the absolute power density of its Raman peak at 1330 nm reaches 3.5 mW/nm, which is double the power density of LH-PCF at this wavelength. The SC power of HH-PCF declines more slowly than that of LH-PCF, with its power density remaining stable for 5.5 h and no significant spectral degradation observed during this period. The absorption peak at 1380 nm plays a critical role in this performance, creating an“optical barrier”within the spectrum that blocks energy transfer to higher-order Raman Stokes bands and causes energy to accumulate at the third Raman Stokes band. Four-wave mixing (FWM) exerts a synergistic effect with the Raman process: the pump light at 1064 nm first generates the first Stokes band at 1112 nm, and FWM then takes the pump light and the first Stokes band as input to produce phase-matched signal and idler photons. At a pump peak power of 26 kW, FWM signals emerge at wavelengths near 1220 nm and 970 nm, with the 1220 nm light experiencing second-order Raman gain. A higher pump peak power of 48 kW shifts the FWM output wavelengths, with signals appearing near 1325 nm and 925 nm. Crucially, the 1325 nm wavelength aligns with the third-order Raman gain band, and the 1380 nm absorption peak prevents further Raman Stokes shift, enabling HH-PCF to efficiently concentrate energy at 1330 nm and overcome the roll-off of traditional Raman gain. Numerical modeling reproduces the spectral evolution process of the SC, with the Raman peak at 1330 nm clearly visible and the simulated absorption dip at 1380 nm prominent in the spectrum, which confirms the mechanism of the hydroxyl-induced“optical barrier”effect. Phase mismatch curves further support the FWM interactions, showing obvious wavelength shifts of FWM outputs with the increase of pump power, and all numerical simulation results are in good agreement with the experimental data. Conclusions Hydroxyl concentration is precisely controlled through the successful combination of VAD and Cl2 dehydration, achieving a hydroxyl mass fraction of 459×10-6 in fused silica. HH-PCF and LH-PCF are fabricated with identical structural parameters, with only their hydroxyl levels differing significantly, which effectively isolates the effect of hydroxyl groups on the nonlinear optical properties of the fibers. High hydroxyl content induces strong optical absorption at 1380 nm with a loss of 32 dB/m, which directly reduces the total SC output power. Crucially, this characteristic absorption endows the fiber with unique nonlinear optical behavior and significantly enhances third-order Raman scattering, leading to a twofold increase in the absolute power density at 1330 nm. The underlying mechanism involves a hydroxyl-induced spectral barrier effect that blocks energy transfer to wavelengths beyond 1380 nm, with FWM and Raman effects interacting synergistically to regulate the spectral evolution process. HH-PCF also exhibits superior output power stability, with its SC performance degrading much more slowly than that of LH-PCF. This work proposes a novel nonlinear optical control strategy based on intentional impurity engineering, where high-hydroxyl fibers are utilized to realize targeted Raman scattering enhancement, holding great application potential in fields such as medical aesthetics and optical communications, including facilitating targeted wavelength therapeutic technologies and enabling feasible Raman amplification for optical signals. Future research efforts should focus on optimizing hydroxyl concentration gradients in the fiber core, combining hydroxyl engineering with microstructural design to further enhance the controllability of nonlinear optical processes, as multi-order Raman scattering management through this hybrid strategy holds considerable promise for the development of advanced nonlinear photonic devices. © 2026 Chinese Laser Press. All rights reserved.
