TI  - A Perspective on hybrid photovoltaic/betavoltaic-zinc-ion batteries for multi-source energy conversion and storage in extreme environments
AU  - Chipura, A.S.
AU  - Dolgopolov, M.V.
AU  - Chen, B.
AU  - San, H.
T2  - Materials Science in Semiconductor Processing
AB  - This Perspective presents a novel materials architecture for a hybrid micro-power systems that synergistically integrates energy harvesting and storage. We introduce a transformative pathway based on β-thermoelectrochemical synergy, where decay heat from nuclear waste (14C) actively manipulates electrochemical kinetics. The proposed hybrid photovoltaic/betavoltaic-zinc-ion battery (PV/BV-ZIB) combines experimentally demonstrated components — including SiC/14C betavoltaic cells (21.31% conversion efficiency from reference Dolgopolov and Chipura (2024)) — with modeled system-level performance. While initial cathode selection considered MoS2 cathodes (173.6 mAhg−1 maximum capacity at 1 Ag−1 according to reference Jiang et al. (2025)), recent research has identified vanadium dioxide (VO2) 3D TiO2/VO2 heterostructure as a superior alternative photocathode, demonstrating under UV irradiation the optimal photo-rechargeable zinc-ion battery (PRZIB) achieved an initial specific capacity of 415.58 mAhg−1 (∼361.51 mAhg−1 in the dark) capacity at 0.1 Ag−1 and 92.3% retention after 500 cycles at 4 Ag−1 (Luo et al., 2025). Our architecture achieves projected metrics including 133.4 mWh/g energy density and 217% enhancement in Zn2+ diffusion coefficient via β-thermal activation. The Embedded Radioisotope Conversion Factor (ERCF) framework provides systematic optimization of radioisotope integration, while radiation-hardened hydrogel electrolytes enable operation across extreme environments (–100 °C to +200 °C). This work not only demonstrates maintenance-free operation potential for over 50 years but also pioneers nuclear waste valorization, aligning with UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) 7, 9, and 12. The study provides a comprehensive roadmap for next-generation micro-power sources, bridging nuclear physics with electrochemistry to enable applications in medical implants, space exploration, and IoT networks where conventional energy storage fails. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
