AU  - Wang, M.
AU  - Liao, X.
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 International Conference on Computer Technology, Digital Media and Communication, ICCDC 2025
AB  - Image schemas, the pre-linguistic, embodied structures of experience such as containment and balance, form the bedrock of human conceptualization and abstract thought. The recent advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) has demonstrated an unprecedented ability to manipulate language, raising a fundamental question: Do these models, trained on vast textual data, develop analogous abstract representations, or is their performance an artifact of sophisticated statistical pattern matching? This paper investigates the extent to which LLMs have acquired representations consistent with two fundamental image schemas: CONTAINER and SOURCE-PATH-GOAL. Through a series of targeted linguistic probes - testing for thematic consistency, novel metaphor interpretation, and logical failures under adversarial conditions - we analyze the behavior of leading LLM architectures. Our findings suggest that while LLMs exhibit a remarkable ability to replicate schema-consistent language on the surface, they demonstrate significant limitations and inconsistencies when faced with novel or logically challenging scenarios. These failures reveal a crucial distinction between the models' data-driven linguistic competence and the embodied, flexible, and inferentially powerful nature of human image-schematic cognition. We conclude that LLMs do not "acquire"image schemas in the human sense but instead construct high-dimensional statistical approximations that, while powerful, lack the robust, generalizable grounding of their biological counterparts. This distinction holds profound implications for the future of artificial intelligence and our understanding of the relationship between language, data, and cognition.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
