TI  - Adversarial attacks and defenses in EEG based Brain Computer Interfaces: A comprehensive survey and future directions
AU  - Sayah Ben Aissa, N.E.H.
AU  - Kerrache, C.A.
AU  - Calafate, C.T.
T2  - Computer Standards and Interfaces
AB  - Brain–Computer Interfaces (BCIs), particularly those based on electroencephalography (EEG), have demonstrated significant potential in assistive technologies, neurorehabilitation, and human–machine interaction. However, their reliance on machine learning models makes them vulnerable to adversarial exploitation. This paper aims to systematically investigate adversarial attacks targeting EEG-based BCI systems and to analyze their implications for reliability and security. Specifically, we examine representative attack scenarios, including adversarial example generation through gradient-based perturbations (e.g., FGSM and PGD), data poisoning during model training, and physical signal perturbations affecting EEG acquisition. These attacks can induce misclassification, degrade system performance, or manipulate user intent decoding without noticeable changes in the signal. By characterizing the objectives, threat models, and impact of such attacks across different stages of the BCI pipeline, this study identifies critical vulnerabilities inherent in EEG-based systems. Furthermore, we review existing defense strategies and highlight current limitations, thereby outlining research directions toward developing robust and secure EEG-based BCI frameworks resilient to adversarial exploitation. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
