AU  - Jacobs, F.J.F.
AU  - Fourie, E.
AU  - Njoya, E.M.
AU  - Makhafola, T.J.
AU  - Brink, A.
T2  - Journal of Molecular Structure
AB  - A series of salicylidene based compounds were designed, via biomimetic strategy that incorporates functional groups present in protein binding sites, as bifunctional chelators for the purpose of coordination with radiopharmaceutical precursors. The functional group range including 1,10-phenanthroline, aniline, exo-2-aminonorbornane, 1-adamantylamine, 1-naphthylmethylamine, taurine, and glucosamine, are found hosted within select protein structures listed on the wwPDB, and were bound to the N,O-ligand backbone to induce structural changes. Detailed crystallographic investigations and cytotoxicity against nine cell lines (tumor and normal cells) are presented to correlate between structural variations and bioactivity. Key observations include the solid-state tautomerism of (1) to (6) in the O-H∙∙∙N form whereas (7) has the keto-enamine N-H∙∙∙O form. Compounds (1) - (7) contain intermolecular hydrogen bonding whereas (3) only has intramolecular bonding. Strong π-π (face-to-face) interactions is observed only for (1). Hirshfeld analysis indicated several O···H interactions with (6) and (7) having percentage contributions of 16.9 % and 31.4 % being significant in the series, and (1) displaying marked N···H interactions (12.7 %). The toxicity potential of compounds (1) - (7) were evaluated against cancer cells (A549 lung, HeLa cervical, HepG2 liver, 4T1, MCF-7 breast, DU145 prostate, Caco-2 colorectal cells) and normal cells (Chang liver, Vero kidney cells). Compound (1) indicated significant activity against all cancer cells except for MCF-7 cells. The selectivity index for (1) displayed good selectivity for HepG2 and Caco-2 cells indicating a promising active compound in its own right as well as a potential bifunctional chelator for radiopharmaceutical development to induce chemotherapeutic versus radiotherapeutic effects. © 2025 The Author(s)
