TI  - Can AI-induced media shape users’ risk perception? Exploring the fallout on local tourism in Lebanon amidst the Gaza conflict
AU  - Bhatt, K.
AU  - AlAshry, M.S.
AU  - Kohli, S.
AU  - Kabia, S.K.
AU  - Seabra, C.
T2  - International Journal of Information Management
AB  - Ignited by long-standing territorial disputes and ideological differences, the Gaza war has implications beyond its immediate effects. As tensions evolved, the role of media in shaping narratives became more crucial, leveraging artificial intelligence to generate misleading narratives. Using Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and Media Dependency Theory (MDT), the present study aims to assess how exposure to AI-generated media content influences risk perception and attitude towards local tourism, focusing on primarily young Lebanese citizens during the Gaza conflict. This research employed a sequential mixed-method approach, combining content analysis of four prominent Arab and non-Arab international online newspapers with a quantitative analysis of survey responses collected from 503 Lebanese citizens. The qualitative findings helped contextualize the AI-induced media environment and empirically ground the study's theoretically derived constructs and their hypothesized relationships. On the other hand, quantitative phase tested these relationships among the key variables. The study revealed that AI-generated misinformation, such as manipulated visuals significantly shaped young users’ risk perception during conflict, amplifying fear and deterring Local tourism in Lebanon. The findings reveal that trust on AI-induced media, when not accompanied by AI-induced media awareness, is associated with heightened anxiety and reduced tourism intentions. These insights underscore the importance of designing transparent and emotionally aware media systems, and of strengthening digital literacy to support informed decision-making in conflict-affected, tourism-dependent economies. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
