TI  - Mamba2Diff: An enhanced diffusion framework for goal-conditioned imitation learning in robotic long-horizon action modeling
AU  - Zhao, G.
AU  - Liu, Q.
AU  - Deng, W.
AU  - Hu, J.
T2  - Knowledge-Based Systems
AB  - Executing long-horizon, high-precision, goal-oriented tasks is essential for enabling robots to perform complex manipulation procedures in dynamic environments. In light of this, Goal-Conditioned Imitation Learning (GCIL) leverages human demonstrations to derive goal-oriented policies, demonstrating significant promise in autonomous robotic manipulation of intricate tasks. However, current GCIL approaches face challenges in capturing both long- and short-term dependencies and in generating consistent behaviors, especially within high-dimensional and dynamic environments. To address these limitations, we propose a novel diffusion-based framework, Mamba2Diff, which aims to improve policy generation by effectively capturing temporal dependencies from multi-modal inputs, thereby enhancing both action consistency and generation accuracy. The core of Mamba2Diff includes the Bidirectional Gated Mamba2 (BDGM) module, which extracts global features through bidirectional information flow, ensuring stability and consistency in long-horizon action modeling. Additionally, a Convolution-Enhanced Gated Recurrent Unit (CGRU) module is incorporated to efficiently capture short-term dependencies, balancing long- and short-term information to further improve generation precision. The effectiveness of Mamba2Diff is evaluated across three simulated environments and a customized real-world robotic platform. The results demonstrate that Mamba2Diff consistently outperforms both diffusion-based and non-diffusion-based baselines in long-horizon manipulation tasks, achieving superior performance in terms of precision, efficiency, and temporal consistency, with significant advantages observed during the later stages of task execution. These findings highlight the model's capacity to generate reliable and accurate robotic behaviors, underscoring its potential for deployment in dynamic, real-world applications. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
