TI  - EQCM-D assisted characterization of polyamide nanofiltration membrane: Mechanistic insights of solutes transport under various pH conditions
AU  - Wang, L.A.
AU  - Yang, Z.
AU  - Guo, H.
AU  - Liu, W.
AU  - Xiao, Q.
AU  - Tang, C.Y.
T2  - Journal of Membrane Science
AB  - Adjusting solution pH, as a powerful manipulation method for nanofiltration process and its separation efficiency, is widely applied for removal of salts, small molecules and micropollutants. Yet some confusing phenomena reported, such as the change of membrane conformation, dominant-ions and mechanisms (steric hindrance versus charge repulsion) competition, hinder our understanding of the transport behavior of these solutes across charged polyamide matrix in complex solution pH and solute conditions. Here we adopt advanced in-situ technology, electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance with dissipation, for monitoring the transformation of polyamide active layer under various pH conditions. A real-time process of membrane resistance, mass and dissipation are recorded simultaneously, denoting a combination change of charged or uncharged, hydration or dehydration and swelling or shrinking of the polyamide backbone over solution pH. The dominant-ions competition is further evaluated through actual nanofiltration test with purposely selected symmetric (sodium chloride) and asymmetric (magnesium chloride and sodium sulfate) salts in feed solution. These fundamental insights regarding membrane transformation and the influence of solution composition on separation performance when adjusting solution pH provides a crucial guidance for precise separation with various aims in the context of nanofiltration. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
