TI  - Quasi-symplectic ADRC hybridization to stabilize a flexible truss for a space telescope
AU  - Rojas, A.
AU  - Sun, L.
AU  - Zhao, X.
AU  - Liu, Y.
T2  - Aerospace Science and Technology
AB  - Space-based telescopes overcome atmospheric disturbances but are constrained by launcher payload limits, motivating deployable flexible truss architectures. Structural flexibility, however, introduces low-frequency vibrations that degrade telescope performance. To explore strategies which mitigates these effects, we study a flexible truss assembled with Stewart platforms to attenuate in a shorter time the residual vibrations after mechanical disturbance. This requires the development of a control system capable to manipulate the Stewart platforms. Then, we propose a quasi-symplectic Active Disturbance Rejection Control (ADRC) framework for vibration mitigation in flexible space trusses actuated by Stewart platforms. This framework combines an Extended State Observer with some concepts from symplectic principles to obtain results that should more accurately represent real-world behavior, considering the discretization inherent in communication among electronic subsystems onboard a spacecraft. Two configurations are analyzed: (1) A flexible truss assembled on a single Stewart platform. This configuration is used to introduce the framework of the quasi-symplectic ADRC. (2) A second configuration is the same flexible truss assembled on the same Stewart platform considered in the first configuration and a second and smaller Stewart platform assembled on another end of the flexible truss. Although in the first configuration is achieved a mitigation of the vibration in a shorter time compared with the setting corresponding to the flexible truss assembled with two Stewart platforms, an interesting result is that the second Stewart platform can operate as a backup system if the main platform is not active, improving the reliability and robustness of the overall stabilization mechanism. © 2026 Elsevier Masson SAS.
