TI  - Decoding Deception: Machine Learning for Fake News Detection in Arab Social Media
AU  - Nimer, M.A.
AU  - Alshraideh, M.
AU  - AlDajani, I.M.
T2  - Adaptivity of Science in Reconciliation Conflict Transformation and Peace Studies
AB  - Currently, social media (SM) platforms are among the most crucial sources of news and information. However, the spread of fake news, false information, and rumors circulated among people in various countries and regions, especially in the Arab world, presents one of the significant challenges faced by users of these platforms. They often lack credibility, transparency, and reliability. Moreover, Fake news refers to intentionally fabricated information disseminated for political, economic, or social purposes. Its spread can lead to conflicts and disputes within or outside the country. It may also propagate hate speech and racism, tarnish reputations, and so on, all of which adversely affect peacebuilding efforts within our communities. In this research study, we present a methodology for detecting fake news using Machine Learning algorithms to enhance peacebuilding efforts and mitigate the spread of fake news, reducing its negative effects on societies. We created a new dataset containing 188 short-text news articles, encompassing both fake and real news on the same topic, from social media platforms (fact-checking websites) circulated in Arab countries and the Middle East. Then, we applied four machine learning algorithms, Decision Tree (DT), Support Vector Machine (SVM), Random Forest (RF), and Naive Bayes (NB), to the dataset. We compared our dataset with two different datasets containing fake and real news. The study results showed that the DT achieved the highest accuracy, approximately 77%, followed by NB at 74%. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
