TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integrated Programmable Diffraction Neural Network Based on Phase-Change Material (Invited)
AU  - Han, Z.
AU  - Gao, C.
AU  - Wang, G.
AU  - Shen, J.
AU  - Cheng, Z.
AU  - Zhou, P.
T2  - Guangxue Xuebao/Acta Optica Sinica
AB  - Objective With the exponential growth of artificial intelligence (AI) in fields such as image recognition, natural language processing, and decision-making systems, the demand for computational power in neural network models is surging. However, traditional electronic computing systems based on the von Neumann architecture are increasingly constrained by the“ memory wall”bottleneck, where the separation of storage and calculation units limits energy efficiency and bandwidth. Consequently, they struggle to meet the real-time inference demands of large-scale deep learning models. Optical neural networks (ONNs) have emerged as a promising next-generation hardware platform due to their inherent parallelism, ultra-high bandwidth, and low latency. Among various ONN implementations, diffractive optical neural networks (DONNs) are particularly attractive for their passive propagation, compact structure, and high computational density. However, most existing on-chip DONNs rely on dielectric materials for phase modulation. These architectures, once fabricated, suffer from fixed weights and lack the reconfigurability required for dynamic tasks or model updates. While some tunable solutions using thermo-optic effects exist, they often incur high power consumption and large footprints. To address these limitations, this study proposes a novel, reconfigurable on-chip diffractive neural network based on the low-loss phase-change material (PCM) Sb2Se3. The primary objective is to demonstrate a high-density, low-power, and programmable optical intelligence chip capable of performing complex classification tasks by leveraging the non-volatile and reversible phase transition properties of Sb2Se3. Methods The proposed device is designed on a silicon-on-insulator (SOI) platform. The core innovation lies in the design of the reconfigurable diffractive unit. We embed the low-loss phase-change material Sb2Se3into silicon waveguides. The diffraction unit is constructed by etching a 220 nm thick silicon waveguide into the substrate and filling it with Sb2Se3. The unit has a thickness of 220 nm, a width of 200 nm, and a fixed length of 5.5 μm, with a period of 500 nm. The phase modulation mechanism relies on the significant refractive index contrast between the crystalline and amorphous states of Sb2Se3. By controlling the crystallization level within the fixed length unit using external methods such as optical or electrical pulses, the optical field mode of the waveguide is altered, thereby modulating the phase of the incident light. To validate the design, we employ both theoretical diffraction models based on the Huygens-Fresnel principle and 3D finite difference time domain (FDTD) simulations. The network architecture is tested on two benchmarks: the Iris dataset and the Fashion-MNIST dataset. For the Iris dataset, a single-layer diffraction network with 128 units is used. For the more complex Fashion-MNIST dataset, a hybrid architecture is adopted, consisting of an electronic convolutional neural network (CNN) front-end for feature extraction and a four-layer optical diffraction network back-end for classification. Results and Discussions The material characterization reveals that Sb2Se3exhibits excellent optical properties in the near-infrared region (1550 nm). Simulation results demonstrate that by varying the amorphous length of the 5.5 μm unit, a full 0‒2π phase coverage is achieved while maintaining an insertion loss below 0.3 dB (Fig. 1).The microscopic interaction between light and the diffraction unit is further elucidated by field distribution simulations. As the material transitions from a fully crystalline state to a partially amorphous state and finally a fully amorphous state, the optical field distribution within the waveguide shifts significantly, confirming the physical mechanism of phase modulation (Fig. 2). For the Iris classification task, the network achieves a high classification accuracy of 96%. The confusion matrix indicates clear decision boundaries with minimal misclassification (Fig. 3). FDTD simulations of the trained passive structure visually verify the inference process: incident light encoded with data from different flower classes undergoes complex interference and is correctly focused onto the designated spatial output detectors (Fig. 4). The specific peaks in the electric field distribution at the output plane perfectly align with the target detector positions for each class, demonstrating the robustness of the physical implementation. For the more challenging Fashion-MNIST dataset, the hybrid optoelectronic network demonstrates strong performance. The 32-input, 10-output optical network, comprising four programmable diffraction layers, successfully classifies the fashion items. The training curves indicate that the testing accuracy stabilizes at approximately 88%, with the training accuracy reaching about 91% (Fig. 5). The system maintains high separability. This result significantly outperforms many reported passive DONN architectures, highlighting the efficacy of the multi-layer reconfigurable design. Conclusions In this study, we have successfully designed and simulated an integrated, programmable diffractive neural network based on the low-loss phase-change material Sb2Se3. By integrating Sb2Se3into standard silicon photonics technology, we realize a diffractive unit capable of precise, non-volatile phase modulation with extremely low insertion loss. The proposed system overcomes the rigidity problem of DONNs, offering a path toward reconfigurable optical computing chips. The network demonstrates superior performance in standard benchmarks, achieving 96% accuracy on the Iris dataset and 88% on the Fashion-MNIST dataset. The physical simulations confirm that the trained phase profiles effectively manipulate light propagation through interference to perform logic and classification operations in the optical domain. The ability to retain weights without static power consumption makes this approach highly suitable for energy-efficient edge computing. In conclusion, the integration of low-loss PCMs with diffractive optical networks represents a significant step forward in the scaling up of silicon photonics for AI. It provides a viable solution for constructing high-density, low-power, and dynamic optical intelligence hardware, paving the way for future large-scale, general-purpose photonic processors. © 2026, Chinese Laser Press. All Rights Reserved.
