AU  - Longhi, J.
AU  - Miklóssy, K.
T2  - Recontextualising Expert Knowledge: The Process, Practices and Aims of Digital Dissemination
AB  - In this chapter, we introduce a project that required fluid recontextualization practices due to its politically sensitive research agenda, namely, extremism. In a time of increasingly polarizing societies where hate speech, intolerance, and fake news have led to a post-truth era, creating trust through credible communication and active engagement with societal actors is imperative. Since the ultimate goal of the project was to raise public awareness and to provide a meaningful contribution to tackling a very central societal problem, we applied a three-dimensional approach, which simultaneously addressed political biases, recognized national specificities, and underlined the necessity of standardized ethical principles of dissemination. Multinational and interdisciplinary collaborations always face varied receptions of research findings across different contexts. We identified the challenge whereby the same type of stakeholders operating in different national or local environments continuously required an elastic calibration of the same scholarly content. The success of creating working communication channels with diverse actors in multiple national contexts relied on the close collaboration, circulation of information on the national political environments, existing networks of individual scholars or their institutions, and the best practices of interacting with target groups. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Rosa Lorés and Pilar Mur-Dueñas; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.
