TY  - JOUR
TI  - Granular motivational interaction and behavioral choice during feeding
AU  - Liu, Q.
AU  - Wang, L.
T2  - Neuron
AB  - Animals inhabit ever-changing environments where multiple needs frequently coexist. The dynamic interplay of diverse motivations enables them to make adaptive behavioral choices for survival and reproduction. This review defines “granular motivational states” and proposes a theoretical framework, “granular motivational interaction orchestrates real-time behavioral choice,” to systematically explore the dynamic interactions of granular motivational states in feeding. Assisted by artificial intelligence (AI), the feeding process is divided into preparation, initiation, maintenance, interruption, and termination phases, orchestrated by discrete granular motivational states such as food seeking, approaching, investigation, sustained eating, satiation, aversion, and non-feeding-related states. Key nuclei, including the arcuate nucleus (ARC), lateral hypothalamus (LH), dorsal raphe (DR), ventral tegmental area (VTA), parabrachial nucleus (PBN), and nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), integrate internal states with environmental cues and encode these granular motivational states. Environmental stressors and internal states disrupt these interactions by altering neural activity and reward encoding. Brain-behavior-physiology quantification, synchronization, and manipulation technologies enable precise characterization of these granular dynamics, providing new perspectives for understanding intelligence and psychiatric disorders. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.
