TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integration of Two-Photon Imaging and Optogenetics: a New Platform for Neuroscience Research (Invited)
AU  - Jiagen, Y.
AU  - Peihua, J.
AU  - Jiajia, C.
AU  - Yao, Z.
AU  - Ke, S.
T2  - Zhongguo Jiguang/Chinese Journal of Lasers
AB  - Significance As the core field to explore the essence of life and the origin of consciousness, brain science is not only related to the underlying mechanism of neural processes such as cognition, memory and emotion, but also has far-reaching significance for the treatment of neurological diseases, the development of brain-like intelligence and the overall breakthrough of life science. However, due to the high complexity of the brain, traditional technologies face significant challenges in observation depth, manipulation accuracy and functional analysis ability, and the two-photon all-optical platform effectively addresses these problems through its unique technical advantages, providing unprecedented technical support for analyzing the complex structure and dynamic function of the brain. Firstly, the two-photon all-optical platform breaks through the technical bottleneck of high-resolution observation of deep nerve tissue in vivo. Traditional optical imaging technology is limited by the scattering and absorption of light in biological tissues, and it is difficult to penetrate thick biological tissues. However, two-photon imaging can significantly reduce the light-induced damage and scattering interference by using the nonlinear excitation characteristics of long-wavelength laser at the focal point, and can realize high-resolution imaging at subcellular scale in living brain tissues. At the same time, the excitation volume is limited to the focal spot area, which greatly reduces the phototoxicity and provides the possibility for long-term in vivo observation. Secondly, the platform realizes the synchronization of high temporal and spatial resolution manipulation and observation of neural activity. One of the core goals of brain science is to understand how neuronal activity encodes, transmits and integrates information. By combining with optogenetics, the two-photon all-optical platform can accurately regulate the electrical activity or calcium signals of specific neurons at the level of single neuron or even subcellular, and record their dynamic responses with high time resolution at the same time, thus realizing the synchronization of“reading”and“writing”of neural activity. This coupling of manipulation and observation enables researchers to shift from“passive recording”to“active intervention”, directly verifying the causal relationship between neuronal activity and behavior and cognitive function, and thus deeply analyzing the operational logic of brain functional networks. In addition, the technological innovation of the two-photon all-optical platform facilitates the realization of functional analysis of large-scale neural networks. By introducing high-speed scanning technology, the platform can greatly improve the imaging flux while maintaining high resolution, and can simultaneously monitor the activity patterns of multiple neurons or brain regions. This ability is very important for studying the dynamic interaction between neurons and provides technical support for analyzing the global function and local mechanism of neural networks. Finally, the two-photon all-optical platform plays a key role in the basic research and application transformation of brain science. At the basic level, it provides a high-precision experimental means for analyzing the molecular mechanism of neuronal activity. At the application level, its precise manipulation ability has been widely used to verify the key nodes in the neural disease model and explore the possibility of targeted neuromodulation therapy. This bridge from“laboratory discovery”to“clinical transformation”not only accelerates the theoretical breakthrough of brain science, but also provides a potential technical path to address the therapeutic challenges of neuropsychiatric diseases. Progress This paper summarizes the development status of two-photon all-optical platform in recent years, and organizes the content from two aspects: imaging and optogenetics stimulation. In the imaging part of the platform, firstly, the basic principle of two-photon imaging is briefly described, and the development of calcium indicators is summarized. Because how to expand the field of view (FOV) while maintaining high resolution as much as possible is an important research direction, including strategies for two-dimensional lateral expansion and three-dimensional axial expansion, the commonly used methods of expanding the FOV are summarized (Table 1). In the part of optogenetics stimulation, it can be divided into scanning stimulation and non-scanning stimulation. In 2018, Yang et al. proposed the spiral scanning stimulation scheme (Fig. 5), while non-scanning holographic stimulation generally combines the time focusing principle to improve the axial resolution, and can be combined with computer-generated holography and generalized phase contrast (Fig. 7). Then, how to avoid optical crosstalk between the imaging system and the stimulation system is discussed, and the safe power budget for biological tissues is analyzed by model. Finally, aiming at the shortcomings of the two-photon all-optical platform at present, the possible improvement direction in the future is put forward. Conclusions and Prospects Two-photon all-optical platform is gradually becoming an important research tool in optogenetics, but it is still necessary to overcome the limitations of imaging FOV and resolution to achieve a larger FOV or deeper imaging range while maintaining high resolution. At present, the light stimulation part is advancing toward the field of single-cell neuronal regulation, and at the same time, it is necessary to develop more sensitive and faster calcium indicators and high-performance optogenetic tools (e.g., fluorescent proteins). In the future, the two-photon all-optical platform will play a greater role in the field of brain function research and open a new door for the development of neuroscience. © 2026 Chinese Laser Press. All rights reserved.
