AU  - Tang, L.
T2  - Zhongguo Jiguang/Chinese Journal of Lasers
AB  - Significance High spatiotemporal resolution characterization is essential for the precise measurement and in-depth understanding of the fundamental physical properties and dynamic processes of the micro- and nano-scale matter. As the cornerstone of time-resolved spectroscopy, ultrafast laser pulses offer temporal resolution ranging from the femtosecond to the attosecond regime. In contrast, quantum mechanical tunneling junctions possess intrinsic atomic-scale spatial resolution, rendering them a complementary capability. The integration of these two approaches has emerged as a powerful route for probing ultrafast dynamics at the atomic and molecular scale, thereby fostering the development of a burgeoning interdisciplinary research field known as ultrafast lightwave electronics. Over the past decade, ultrafast light-coupled tunneling junctions have enabled the direct observation of electron tunneling, charge transfer, and molecular dynamics with unprecedented spatiotemporal resolution, effectively bridging the gap between ultrafast optics and nanoelectronics. Progress Ultrafast light-coupled tunneling junction technology, which integrates ultrafast laser pulses with tunneling junctions, has achieved a breakthrough in attaining femtosecond temporal and atomic-scale spatial resolution. Advances in this field can be summarized from three dimensions: fundamental mechanisms, application expansion, and device development, as detailed in the following three sections. First, the physical fundamentals of ultrafast light-coupled tunneling junctions have evolved from early weak-field perturbation descriptions based on optical rectification and the Tien-Gordon model to the non-perturbative strong-field regime under the Keldysh theoretical framework. This evolution has significantly advanced the understanding of nonlinear quantum tunneling dynamics. The maturation of optical pump-probe operational methodologies, such as terahertz-scanning tunneling microscope (STM) and visible/ near-infrared-STM, has facilitated femtosecond time-resolved current detection via the modulation of tunneling currents by ultrashort pulses, while mitigating thermal interference. Key advancements include the quantitative characterization of photon-assisted tunneling processes and field-driven tunneling processes, as well as the precise control over optical waveforms via carrier-envelope phase stabilization, laying a solid foundation for high signal-to-noise ratio measurements. Second, this technology has enabled the characterization of ultrafast dynamics with atomic-scale resolution in semiconductors, plasmonic nanostructures, and single-molecule systems. In semiconductors, it has overcome the spatial limitations of conventional spectroscopic techniques, enabling real-time imaging of carrier relaxation, defect-state dynamics, and surface potential fluctuations. In plasmonic nanostructures, it has unveiled localized surface plasmon resonance-induced collective electron oscillations and coherent phonon processes, offering insights into nonlinear responses under field enhancement. For single-molecule studies, it has enabled the manipulation of ultrafast processes such as conformational changes, electronic coherence, and vibrational relaxation, advancing the field of molecular electronics. These applications not only enhance spatiotemporal resolution but also decode multidimensional energy-space-time information through multimodal integration, such as terahertz scanning tunneling spectroscopy. Finally, leveraging the principles of ultrafast light-driven on-chip devices, a variety of high-performance optoelectronic devices have been developed, enabling signal processing and logic operations in the petahertz frequency range. These devices translate ultrafast dynamics research into practical technologies, bridging the frequency gap between electronics and photonics, and providing new platforms for ultrafast communication and quantum computing. Conclusions and Prospects By integrating femtosecond lasers with nanoscale tunneling detection, ultrafast light-coupled tunneling junction technology has successfully overcome the inherent trade-off between temporal and spatial resolutions in conventional measurement techniques, achieving simultaneous femtosecond-scale temporal resolution and atomic-level spatial resolution. Evolving from initial proof-of-concept demonstrations, this technology now enables precise analysis of ultrafast processes such as semiconductor carrier dynamics, plasmonic coherent oscillations, and single-molecule quantum coherence, and has further catalyzed the development of novel optoelectronic devices. However, its advancement faces three major challenges: 1) the system’s heavy reliance on the complex coupling between STM and femtosecond lasers, leading to high equipment costs and operational complexity; 2) thermal effects induced by visible/near-infrared excitation, which interfere with ultrafast signal extraction; 3) limitations in the terahertz frequency band imposed by source performance, often confining temporal resolution to the the picosecond level, with near-field distributions susceptible to nanostructural inhomogeneity. Collectively, these factors impede the translation of this technology from basic research to practical applications. Future breakthroughs are anticipated to focus on four key dimensions: 1) broadening the laser source repertoire to include midinfrared lasers and optical frequency combs, thereby enhancing coupling specificity with targeted energy levels; 2) developing multimodal measurement techniques that synchronously acquire tunneling current and spectroscopic signals to reveal the full dynamic picture of complex systems; 3) deepening fundamental investigations into quantum plasmonic effects within nanogaps to elucidate their ultrafast dynamics; 4) extending application scenarios to complex environments such as biomolecules in solution. Realizing these goals necessitates interdisciplinary collaborative innovation, including the development of non-equilibrium theoretical models, the optimization of laser modulation strategies, and advancements in batch fabrication processes for solid-state nanogaps. Through sustained efforts, ultrafast light-coupled tunneling junction technology is poised to become a critical bridge connecting ultrafast optics and quantum materials, offering transformative measurement platforms for fields including petahertz optoelectronics, quantum sensing, and biomedical research. © 2026 Chinese Laser Press. All rights reserved.
