AB  - Purpose. To analyse how visual labels used by fact-checking platforms contribute to the construction of meaning around disinformation, through a comparative study of Spain and Portugal. Methodology. A mixed-method approach was applied, combining quantitative and qualitative content analysis of 823 fact-checks from Maldita.es and Polígrafo over a three-month period. Graphic, chromatic, and semantic elements of the labels were examined. Results and Conclusions. Common use of red to indicate falsehood was identified, along with cultural differences in truth classification. Both platforms visually reinforce semantic categorisation, confirming that visual tags helps with public understanding of disinformation levels. Singularities. This study demonstrates that visual labels are crucial tools for effectively conveying the truthfulness of information, with implications for media literacy and countering disinformation. © 2026, Universidad de Alicante. All rights reserved.
