TI  - FUTURE‑PROOFING EMERGING TECHNOLOGIES (ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE) IN SUB‑SAHARAN AFRICAN DEMOCRACY
AU  - Uzuegbunam, C.E.
T2  - The Routledge Companion to Digital Media and Democracy
AB  - The rapid advancement of emerging technologies, particularly artificial intelligence (AI), has introduced new challenges and opportunities for African democracies and democratic citizenship. This chapter aims to provide insights and recommendations on how to future-proof these technologies. Existing concerns with big data have included the impact of data manipulation for purposes such as elections, privacy and surveillance, citizens’ autonomy and self-determination, and government operations, both online and offline. Recent elections in sub-Saharan African countries such as Nigeria, Kenya, and South Africa witnessed the use of AI-generated; the 2023 Nigerian elections, for instance, saw the use of AI-generated voice recordings, which were later confirmed to be deepfakes. Emerging technologies such as AI can potentially undermine democratic processes by nudging people into promoting third-party agendas through applications such as deepfakes, voice cloning, and algorithmic influencing. However, this chapter does not privilege only the gloomy side of the coin. The chapter explores questions such as: What forms of agency and possibilities do emerging technologies offer ordinary citizens to challenge and subvert both traditional oppressive systems and new digital capitalist structures? How can emerging technologies such as AI be harnessed to foster transparency, accountability, and citizen participation? What ethical considerations need to be taken into account? What contextual limitations might still pose a stumbling block? © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Zizi Papacharissi; individual chapters, the contributors.
