TY  - JOUR
TI  - Parallel Sampling Attention Model for Real-Time Robotic Disk Disassembly Planning
AU  - Wei, J.
AU  - Liu, C.
AU  - Jian, Y.
AU  - Chen, X.
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering
AB  - As cloud computing expands, data centers increasingly rely on robotic systems to efficiently disassemble millions of hard disk drives for secure, periodic data erasure and reuse. Managing such large-scale, dynamic operations requires controlling robots under real-time constraints - posing practical challenges in path planning due to shifting locations, limited perception, and the need for coordinated execution between mobile chassis and manipulator. To tackle these demands, we present a Parallel Sampling Attention Model (PSAM), a deep learning architecture designed for high-throughput, real-time decision-making in industrial disassembly scenarios. PSAM introduces a parallel decoding mechanism that generates diverse candidate paths in a single forward pass, enabling efficient path planning. The solutions are segmented and optimized using a genetic-inspired refinement process, while a redundant decoding strategy enhances solution robustness by balancing quality and planning speed. We deploy PSAM on a real-world robotic disassembly platform, achieving a 37% improvement in operational efficiency and maintaining a 94% task success rate. This work contributes to scalable, intelligent automation for next-generation data center operations, with broad potential for real-time robotic path planning and scheduling in industrial environments. Note to Practitioners - This work was motivated by the practical need to automate the disassembly of millions of hard disk drives in large-scale data centers, where secure data erasure and hardware reuse are critical. Traditional robotic planning systems often struggle with frequent location changes, limited real-time perception, and the complexity of coordinating the chassis and manipulator. To address these challenges, we developed a deep reinforcement learning-based planning method that generates multiple feasible task paths in parallel and efficiently refines them. This approach, called PSAM, enables robots to respond with low latency to dynamic task environments while maintaining a strong balance between planning quality and computational speed. The method has been validated in both simulated and real data center scenarios, showing strong potential for real-time industrial deployment. However, the current system relies on accurate initial perception and working zone assignment, which may limit performance in noisier environments. Future work will focus on integrating this method with uncertain sensor data and more complex task constraints. Beyond hard disk drive disassembly, PSAM may also benefit other high-throughput robotic applications such as sorting, inspection, or repair. © 2004-2012 IEEE.
