TI  - Exploring the Information Disorder Framework: Health-Related Fake News, Bullshit, and Their Impact on COVID-19 Protective Measures and Vaccination Intent
AU  - Secară, E.-C.
AU  - Opre, N.-A.
T2  - North American Journal of Psychology
AB  - This paper examines vulnerability to health-related misinformation and disinformation, as proposed by the Information Disorder Framework, and their pandemic-related associations with COVID-19-specific variables, including belief in the virus’s anthropogenic origin, adherence to protective measures (actual vs. fake), and vaccination intent. Across three studies, we used repeated and single-measure approaches to assess vulnerability to full-length fake news articles and health-related “bullshit” (i.e., randomly generated articles). Exploratory and confirmatory factor analyses identified distinct factors for trust in real versus fake news, without differentiating between misinformation and disinformation or between fake news and health-related bullshit. Greater vulnerability to fake news was associated with intuitive rather than analytical thinking and was linked to lower media discernment, reduced ability to discriminate actual from fake COVID-19 protective measures, and lower vaccination intent. These findings suggest that, although the Information Disorder Framework is useful for classification in educational and policy contexts, its categories may not correspond to distinct cognitive processes underlying fake news vulnerability. © NAJP.
