TY  - JOUR
TI  - Anyanwu and Doro: Fluid Identities and the Struggle for Power in Octavia Butler’s Wild Seed
AU  - Princess, M.G.
AU  - Latha, R.
T2  - Neocolonialism: Re-Presenting Narratives, Realities and Responses
AB  - This study examines the theme of fluid identity in Wild Seed by Octavia Butler where it explores the complexities of identity and power. Fluid identity refers to an identity that is constantly in flux or constantly evolving. It explores the ways in which an individual’s self-perception and identity are constructed through the intricate interplay of social, cultural and personal factors. Mostly, this concept is analysed under queer studies suggesting that identity is not fixed but flexible. This paper takes a different turn by not falling under queer ideologies, but how the protagonist, Anyanwu shapeshifts in order to survive the prejudices and dominance. She has the superpower of transcending traditional boundaries of race, gender and even species. This quality gives a space for her to exist within various communities without being persecuted or recognised as different. Her ability to heal, nurture and protect herself is the foundation of her power, which has allowed her to adapt and survive for centuries. In contrast, Doro’s power relies on his capacity to shift his consciousness into different bodies. This action not only spares him from death but also reinforces his control over others. This exchange of bodies draws attention to a distinct form of fluidity where it is used for the purpose of manipulation and subjugation. His main aim is to control Anyanwu and use her as a breeding machine for his project, which specifies in creating superhumans. Despite the superpowers that Anyanwu has, she is still a black woman, which stands as a reason for her subjugation. Butler’s exploration of these two characters delves into the intersection of race, gender and power where it brings in a nuanced portrayal of how fluid identities can either empower or enslave. © 2024 selection and editorial matter, Dr. L. Santhosh Kumar, Ms. Minu A., Dr. Barnashree Khasnobis, Dr. Preetha M. and Dr. Merrin R. S.; individual chapters, the contributors.
