TY  - JOUR
TI  - The KGB’s Secret Channel and Its Actors, 1969–81
AU  - Petrov, N.
T2  - West German Ostpolitik, the Soviet Union, and East-West Détente in Europe
AB  - The foreign policy pursued by Tsarist Russia up to 1917 and that followed by its Soviet successor may be characterized as adventurist and devious in the extreme. Both policies made use of methods such as bribery, blackmail, and intimidation of adversaries perceived as susceptible. They typically included a tendency toward secret agreements, intrigue, and manipulation. In their reliance on scientific laws of social development and their materialist-philosophical basis, Soviet leaders believed in the existence and efficacy of secret mechanisms and driving forces that could be employed to change the international situation. What was more, they believed in conspiracy theories and a widespread network of agreements directed against the USSR. This may explain, at least in part, the Kremlin’s preference for secret activities at the “ideological front.” In addition, disinformation was used by the Soviets to create a more acceptable and more humane image of the USSR and was an integral part of the arsenal of Soviet foreign political propaganda from the mid-1920s. The goals and objectives of Soviet disinformation policy were clear. John Barron devoted a separate chapter to them in his illuminating book on the KGB.37 Linking the Soviet brand of disinformation to the principles of Leninism, Barron reminds his readers of what the Soviet leader had to say on the admissibility of “all sorts of cunning, schemes and stratagems [. . .] to employ illegal methods and conceal the truth”38 for the sole purpose of accomplishing the communist task. In addition to enhancing the USSR’s image in the West, Soviet disinformation pursued a global goal—fostering latent differences and animosities between “imperialist countries” and undermining people’s trust in their leadership. Actual objectives of the disinformation campaigns the USSR launched in the West included discrediting specific Western politicians, exerting influence on other countries’ foreign policy; manipulating Western countries’ assessments of Soviet foreign and domestic policies, and concealing miscarriages on the part of the KGB’s secret intelligence work. Methods used by the Soviet intelligence services for the achievement of these goals ranged from the dissemination of diverse rumors and the publication of fake reports by agents active in the West to the manipulation of documents and photos and the blackmailing and even murder of individuals who were felt to be inimical or dangerous to the USSR. The involvement of the KGB in Brezhnev’s German policy had different consequences. First, a relatively inconspicuous person had to be put in charge of the covert contacts. Second, it was necessary to grant agents the right to leave the Soviet Union at will and to stay in the target country for prolonged periods, which, in view of the stringent Soviet travel restrictions and the need to impose total control on its citizens, drastically reduced the number of potential candidates. Only one government agency was entitled to grant its emissaries the privilege of leaving the country relatively frequently and remaining in a foreign country for any length of time. The isolation of this agency and the nature of its work posed a specific occupational hazard for its personnel. They were prone to see their own organization as a “state within the state,” with its own unique rules that set it—and them—apart from all other government agencies. At the same time, the USSR’s party leadership encouraged the Chekists not to be queasy in their choice of their means. The view Brezhnev uttered in his report to the Twenty-Fifth Congress of the CPSU that “anything is morally justified in our society that serves the interests of Communism”39 resonated powerfully with the Chekists. It was especially significant that, in the KGB, the functions of the secret services and foreign political disinformation were combined into an organic whole. Copyright © Bloomsbury Publishing, 2026.
