AB  - The escalating prevalence of spoofing attacks with active manipulation intentions poses severe challenges in ensuring the security and trustworthiness of localization using global navigation satellite system (GNSS). Many non-GNSS sensors that are immune to GNSS spoofing have been considered in multisensor structures to compensate the unavailability of the GNSS measurements. However, current sensor fusion solutions are not specifically designed to ensure the performance of localization without sacrificing security. In this article, a spoofing-resilient factor graph localization (SRFGL) framework is designed to integrate multisensor measurements to defend against GNSS spoofing attacks while simultaneously recovering unbiased measurements and trustworthy positioning solutions. Specific countermeasures, including the adaptive spoofing detection and identification, spoofing mitigation based on spoofing profile estimation, and adaptive error modeling, are embedded within a whole factor graph structure to enhance the capability of state recovery against the intrusion by spoofing interference. Results using practically collected spoofing-scenario datasets demonstrate that the proposed solution can achieve spoofing-resilient localization under the multisensor integration architecture. Moreover, the flexibility of the proposed structure makes it easy to be seamlessly integrated into different GNSS-based localization instruments and equipment, contributing to proactive protection against the risk raised by GNSS spoofing. © 2026 IEEE. All rights reserved.
