TI  - Review on Full-Process Mechanization in Drill-and-Blast Railway Tunnels
AU  - Cao, G.
AU  - Li, G.
AU  - Li, N.
AU  - Wang, W.
AU  - Yuan, Y.
T2  - Railway Standard Design
AB  - To systematically analyze the research progress in full-process mechanization of railway tunnels constructed by the drill-and-blast method, this study reviews current mechanized construction practices and identifies key challenges in achieving complete mechanization. Future development directions are proposed accordingly. The results show that: 1) mechanical equipment should be deployed along nine operational lines, with targeted configurations for adverse geological conditions. 2) Although investment increases after mechanized configuration, the drill-and-blast tunnel construction achieves the goals of accelerating progress by 10% and reducing personnel by 48%. 3) Current mechanization levels remain low in processes such as explosive charging, invert construction, waterproof sheet installation, rebar binding, and deep central trench excavation in cold-region tunnels. This necessitates further research into technologies such as mechanized charging, prefabricated inverts, automated construction of waterproof sheets with reinforcing steel, and mechanized trench forming. 4) For mechanized construction in tunnels with different design speeds, vehicle passing issues can be addressed by three principles: No mutual adjustments between structure and equipment, equipment adaptation to structural constraints (drilling jumbo width reduced to 2. 6 m, loader width to 2. 8 m), and coordinated adaptations between both (drilling jumbo rig width to 2. 6 m, loader to 2. 3 m, dump truck to 2. 2 m, with the structure widened by 2. 1 m). Turnaround issues can be solved by increasing the size of turnaround niches. 5) For small-cross-section tunnels, narrow-body equipment and integrated machine solutions require further research. The operational efficiency of equipment such as drilling jumbos, mechanized charging devices, drill-grout-bolt integrated machines, wet-mix shotcrete manipulators, and waterproofing-rebar work trolleys needs further improvement. 6) The adaptability of new energy equipment needs enhancement, and the tunnel belt conveyor mucking system requires improvement. 7) Further research is needed on intelligent construction organization and equipment system control theories, aiming to develop more intelligent equipment, build intelligent management and control platforms, and ultimately achieve unmanned tunnel construction. © 2026, Editorial Office of Railway Standard Design. All rights reserved.
