TI  - Hazard communications using interactive computer based training techniques
AU  - Jones, P.
T2  - The Management of Construction Safety and Health
AB  - From their inception, computers were touted for their capacity to manipulate, store and retrieve information (data). There is growing recognition that computers can be used very effectively for direct, interactive training. This paper describes a project with two goals: first, to create an electronic version of a hazardous chemicals database that improves access to information and second, to increase the relevance of the database by linking it to frequently used training modules. HazCom Interactive runs on personal computers and delivers information on hazardous chemicals from the International Chemical Safety Card (ICSC) database developed by the International Programme on Chemical Safety (IPCS). The ICSC uses standard phrases in multiple languages to describe the characteristics of ~1,200 hazardous chemicals. Pictographs that correspond to many of the standard phrases were created and are displayed to reinforce text. Audio files can be activated to ‘read’ the textual descriptions of the chemicals. HazCom Interactive provides specific, high quality information and is linked to relevant training modules on ventilation, respirators and PPE. The training modules are designed to be highly interactive and generally operate like games. In the project’s final phase the software will be evaluated for its efficacy in actual training situations. © 2000 by Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
