TI  - Kinematic analysis and integration of vision algorithms for a mobile manipulator employed inside a self-driving laboratory
AU  - Sulaiman, S.
AU  - Jensen, T.B.
AU  - Bengtson, S.H.
AU  - Bøgh, S.
T2  - International Journal of Intelligent Robotics and Applications
AB  - Recent advances in robotics and autonomous systems have expanded the role of robots in laboratory environments, enabling automated synthesis, scalable reaction workflows, and collaborative tasks in self-driving laboratories (SDLs). In this work, we present a development of a motion planning scheme for a mobile manipulator specifically designed to validate feasibility of integrating kinematic modelling with vision-based tracking in autonomous laboratory settings. The manipulator’s kinematic structure is modelled using the Denavit–Hartenberg (DH) convention, and inverse kinematics solutions are employed to achieve precise and adaptive motion control. To support robust object interaction, vision-based algorithms are intentionally incorporated to perform real-time detection and pose estimation, guiding the manipulator in dynamic grasping and tracking tasks. Our approach combines feature-based detection with homography-driven pose estimation, leveraging depth information to represent object orientation as a 2D planar projection within 3D space. This design choice enables the system to accommodate variations in object orientation while ensuring reliable autonomous manipulation. By demonstrating the integration of kinematic modeling with vision-based initialization, this study highlights a pathway towards scalable, reproducible, and collaborative experimentation in next-generation chemical laboratories. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.
