T2  - BioMed Bots Assurance: Securing Robotics, Healthcare, and IT Convergence
AB  - The autonomous wheelchair can perform complex tasks accurately and efficiently using a computer vision algorithm to read and react to visual inputs. Actuators for physical manipulation, a machine learning model for object recognition and gesture understanding, and a camera for visual perception are essential parts. Moreover, computer vision integration improves the wheelchair’s automation. It is capable of collecting information from its surroundings, modifying its movement plan in response to immediate feedback, and working with human operators. The goal of this research work is to develop a human-body gesture recognition system that can track and identify wheelchair movements in real time, and then transfer the identified gestures to an Arduino board for further processing. By using image processing to detect and analyze human body landmarks, the webcam can capture frames and determine the whereabouts of people inside a designated region of wheelchair landmarks. The digital representations of the resultant human body states are transmitted to an Arduino board through serial communication. A 3D model design and simulation of a wheelchair, along with MATLAB code, is showcased in real-time scenarios using V-REP software. The results in simulation and in real time are more than 95% accurate.
