TY  - JOUR
TI  - Research on Collaborative Governance Strategies of AIGC False Information Based on a Tripartite Evolutionary Game
AU  - Junping, Q.
AU  - Zhang, T.
AU  - Xu, Z.
T2  - Library and Information Service
AB  - [Purpose/Significance] It is of great significance to explore the factors influencing the collaborative governance of artificial intelligence-generated content (AIGC) false information and its evolution law, in order to promote the sustainable and healthy development of false information governance system. [Method/Process] This article constructed an evolutionary game model of “user-social media-government”, solved the evolutionarily stable strategies using the Jacobi matrix and Lyapunov’s first method, and finally conducted numerical simulation and sensitivity analysis. [Result/Conclusion] The study shows that: (1) The strategies of users, social media, and government are influenced by their initial willingness. (2) The control willingness, the punishment, and the control effectiveness of social media and the government all affect the user’s strategies. (3) The government’s willingness to regulate, regulatory efficacy, and punishment, the governance costs and benefits of social media, and the incremental governance success rate brought about by users’ participation in collaborative governance all affect the social media’s governance strategies. (4) The government’s regulatory costs, additional credibility gains, and the incremental success rate of governance brought about by user participation in collaborative governance all affect the government’s regulatory strategies. (5) The phenomenon of “free-riding” exists between the government and social media. (6) Users are more sensitive to the punishment of social media than the government and more sensitive to the control effectiveness of the government than social media. Based on this, the research proposes optimization strategies to improve the collaborative governance system of AIGC false information. © LIS Press Co., Ltd.
